r10rggen9 purpose-built Surelog goldens — file -> law it pins

Generate a golden with
  tools/probe/r10rggen9golden.sh <this dir> <file>.sv
and ours with
  tools/probe/r10rggen9ours.sh   <this dir> <file>.sv
then uhdm-cmp the two .uhdm files (an EMPTY cmp output means equal), or diff
/tmp/r10rggen9_ours.txt against the golden dump.  (r10rggen9golden.sh leaves a
slpp_all/ behind; it is scratch, never commit it.)

t5.sv  § selCallBin + § typedCallFoldTs — five one-difference siblings of
       rggen_address_decoder:18 `bit [WIDTH-LSB-1:0] END_ADDRESS =
       calc_end_address(START_ADDRESS, BYTE_SIZE)`, whose elaborated
       param_assign Rhs is BIN at the SELECT width in golden and was decimal
       UINT in ours.  The encoding follows the function's RETURN EXPRESSION,
       not the declared type:
         return e[WIDTH-1:LSB]   , param bit [5:0] -> BIN:000101 size 6
         return e[WIDTH-1:LSB]   , param int       -> BIN:000101 size 6 (!)
         return (W-LSB)'(n + 16) , param bit [5:0] -> UINT:20    size 6
         return 20               , param bit [5:0] -> UINT:20    size 6
         return <whole local>    , param bit [5:0] -> UINT:20    size 6
       and EVERY one of the five carries a ts ref to the parameter's own
       declared typespec (bit_typespec / int_typespec), which our const-call
       fold only attached on the predefined-integer arm.
       Cause (UHDM templates/ExprEval.cpp): the part_select / indexed_part_
       select / bit_select reduce arms are the only ones that build
       `BIN:<bits>` sized to the SELECT; evalFunc's return tail re-masks that
       constant only for a logic_typespec return, so a bit[]/int return hands
       the select's own binary value straight to the caller.
       STILL OPEN in this file: golden SHARES the body literal of `return 20;`
       with the fold (the `constant@38:12` carrying the C3_CALL parameter's
       bit_typespec) — the EvalFunc-leftover family, not modelled.

t7.sv  the compound-assignment WRITE-BACK, FULLY DECODED (not implemented) —
       see the § selCallBin sibling note in ParamEmit and the block-3 commit
       message.  Five predictions, all confirmed by golden:
         `a += 1` under `while (a < 3)`   -> UINT:4   (1 * 2^2)
         `a += 1` under `for i in 0..4`   -> UINT:16  (1 * 2^4)
         `a += 1` under `for i in 0..63`  -> UINT:0   (wrapped)
         `a += 1` under `for i in 0..69`  -> UINT:0   (wrapped)
         `b -= 2` twice from b = 5        -> INT:0    (5-2=3, then 3-3=0)
       The mechanism is UHDM ExprEval::setValueInInstance (~5021 + the opType
       tail ~5395): `c = any_cast<constant*>(rhsexp)` IS the assignment's own
       literal object in the tree, the op arms OVERWRITE its VpiValue with
       `prev <op> val`, and the param_assign that records the LHS's value is
       then given THAT SAME OBJECT.  So from the second execution on
       prev == val and `a += <lit>` DOUBLES the literal every iteration, and
       the uint64 arithmetic (masked at the literal's own VpiSize) wraps to
       ZERO once the loop is long enough.  rggen_or_reducer:30
       `list_index += 1` is UINT:0 in golden for exactly that reason — the
       function is evaluated once per rggen_or_reducer instance and the DEF
       listing's literal object is shared across all of them.

t8.sv  § ifacePortVarHighConn — a port actual reaching an interface signal
       through the enclosing module's own INTERFACE PORT
       (rggen_register_common:44 `.i_address (register_if.address)`).  Golden
       binds the interface's VARIABLE OBJECT as vpiHighConn (a logic_var named
       `address`, located at the interface declaration and re-fullnamed
       `<inst>.<formal>.<signal>`); a SELECT on the signal
       (`mif.address[3:0]`) is DISCARDED and binds the whole variable.  The
       control in the same file is a LOCALLY INSTANTIATED interface
       (`ifc u_if(); .i_valid(u_if.valid)`), which keeps the hier_path in
       golden as well as in ours.

t9.sv  § ifacePortVarHighConn, the negative half.  Three `mid*` modules that
       differ in one thing each:
         A  `ifc.mp mif`  (interface port WITH a modport)  -> logic_var
         B  `ifc bif`     (interface port, NO modport)     -> hier_path
         C  `pifc pif`    (no modport, members are the interface's own PORTS,
                           the OneNetInterf / OneNetModPortGeneric /
                           OneNetRange shape) -> hier_path
       so the MODPORT is the distinguisher: Surelog only resolves the member
       to an object when the modport declared which signals are visible.  The
       first cut of the law omitted this gate and regressed those three tests;
       t9 is byte-identical to golden with it.

ta.sv  § caBareEnumFold — TRIED AND REVERTED (see the NOTE in ExprEmit around
       the `pkg::VALUE` fold).  rggen_register_common:104 `assign
       register_if.status = RGGEN_OKAY;` folds in golden even though the read
       is a BARE wildcard-imported enum member; ta pairs bare vs
       package-qualified reads against an interface-signal LHS and a plain-net
       LHS, plus a ternary, a cast and a plain package parameter:
         `assign sm.status = OKAY`   -> LOC-LESS constant BIN:00 size 2 ct 3
         `assign w0       = OKAY`    -> the same (so the LHS is irrelevant)
         `assign sm.status2 = p::ERR`-> constant AT the written span
         `assign w2 = E ? ERR : OKAY`-> loc-less INT:2 size 2
         `assign w3 = status_e'(..)` -> loc-less UINT:2 size 2
         `assign w4 = PC`            -> constant at the span, with the
                                        package int_typespec
       The blocker is not the predicate: golden keeps the ref_obj in the DEF
       listing and the Rggen elab gen tree carries golden objects of BOTH
       shapes, so a cont_assign object shared across our two views cannot
       satisfy both.  Per-view cont_assign objects are the prerequisite.
